course: reflection
lesson: 12
title: Parents shouting, parents splitting: it's not your fault
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: When things go wrong between grown-ups at home, children very often decide, quietly, in bed, that it must be because of them, and that if they are good enough they can fix it.
explain: Through the wall Maisie heard Mum and Dad shouting again, a door slammed and Dad's car left; she lay awake thinking, "Is it 'cause I was bad at tea? If I'm really, really good, maybe they'll stop," and for weeks she was extra quiet and tidy, with a sore tummy every morning.
explain: The truth is that grown-ups argue about grown-up things like money, tiredness and work; a child does not cause it and a perfect child cannot stop it, so that heavy job was never hers to carry.
explain: What helped was telling Mum she had heard, and Mum saying clearly, "It's not your fault"; she still felt worried when voices rose, but she knew it wasn't her job to fix it.
explain: And when home ever feels scary, like a parent who drinks and turns loud and frightening, or a brother who really hurts you, it is not a secret to keep: tell a trusted adult such as a teacher, a gran or a children's helpline.
fact: Parents argue about grown-up things like money, tiredness and work; it is never caused by the child, and a child being perfect cannot stop it.
fact: "Rowing" is a UK word for arguing; "it's not on you" means it is not your responsibility.
fact: When parents split up ("separate"), children often hope it will go back to how it was; over months, a new normal in two homes can feel okay, even good.
fact: Having your own things in both homes and a calendar showing where you'll be can help after a split.
fact: Children should not have to choose between parents; where a child lives ("custody") is a grown-up decision, and "I love you both, I don't want to choose" is a fair thing to say.
fact: Drinking too much alcohol can change how a person acts; if a child feels scared at home because of it, it is not his fault and he should tell a trusted adult.
fact: Money worries come from bills, jobs and prices, not from one child's shoes; children often blame themselves for things they did not cause.
fact: Depression is an illness that makes a person very sad and tired for a long time; it is not the child's fault, a child cannot cure it, and doctors can help.
fact: New stepparents take time; feeling angry at first is normal, and doing small things together can help people get on.
fact: Being hurt at home is not okay, even by a brother or sister; play-fighting that really hurts and scares you is not play, and telling a grown-up keeps you safe.
fact: "Made redundant" means losing a job because there is no work for it; it is not anyone's fault, and families can get through a move together.
fact: When parents get back together after a split, trusting that things will stay good can take time; mixed feelings, happy and scared at once, are normal.
exercise: Maisie thinks her parents argue because she was bad at tea. What is true? | Grown-ups argue about grown-up things; it is never caused by the child | If she is perfect from now on and eats all her tea, they will stop arguing | Children are usually the main cause of parents' rows | She should go in and tell them to stop shouting
exercise: What helped Maisie? | Telling Mum she heard, and Mum saying it was not her fault | Trying to be perfect at home so that they wouldn't fight any more | Keeping it secret and listening at the wall every single night | Hiding in her room every evening until the rows ended for good
exercise: Josh's dad moved to a flat across town. After six months, how did things feel? | Like a new kind of normal, with his own stuff in both homes | Exactly as bad as the very first day | Back to how it was, with both parents together | So bad he refused to visit his dad ever again
exercise: What did NOT help Josh? | Each parent asking him what the other one said | Each parent telling him they loved him, whatever happened | A calendar showing where he would be | Keeping his things in both homes
exercise: Mum asked Ava, "Who do you want to live with?" What is a fair answer? | "I love you both, I don't want to choose." | "Whoever buys me the best things." | Tell each parent it's them, to keep both happy | Run away so nobody has to choose
exercise: Leo's dad drinks a lot of beer at weekends and gets loud and scary. What should Leo do? | Tell a trusted adult like a teacher, because he's scared | Keep it secret forever to protect his dad | Hide the beer so his dad can't find it | Try to calm his dad down all by himself, every single weekend
exercise: Holly heard "We can't afford ANYTHING!" and decided it was because of her new school shoes. What is true? | Money worries are about bills and jobs, not one child's shoes | Her shoes really did cause the family's money problems | She should never ask for anything ever again | She should hide her worn-out shoes so Mum doesn't spend
exercise: Max's mum stays in bed and is sad all the time. Max tells jokes to cheer her up. What is true? | Depression is an illness; it's not his fault, and doctors can help | If he is funny enough, her illness will go away | She is sad because Max isn't helping enough | Mums who stay in bed just don't love their children
exercise: What helped Max's mum get better? | Max told his gran, who helped Mum see a doctor | Max made even more breakfasts and told more jokes | Max stopped talking at home so that she could rest | Max kept it all a secret from everyone, even Gran
exercise: Sophie's new stepdad is strict and she wishes he'd never moved in. What helped them get on a bit better? | Family talks with Mum and Saturday pancakes with him | Refusing to speak to him for a whole year | Telling him every single day that he's not her real dad | Breaking his rules to show who's boss
exercise: Kian's big brother hits him hard when their parents are out, and Kian hides the bruises. What is true? | It's not okay, and telling a grown-up keeps him safe | It's normal play-fighting, so he should toughen up | If he tells, it will always get worse, so stay quiet | Brothers are allowed to hit if parents aren't home
exercise: Mia's dad lost his job and they have to move. What does "made redundant" mean? | Losing a job because there is no work for it | Being sacked from a job for doing something wrong | Choosing to stop working so as to stay at home | Getting a better job with more pay in a new town
exercise: What helped Mia with the move? | Visiting the new school and keeping in touch with friends | Blaming her dad every day for losing his job and making them move | Refusing to speak to anyone at the new school | Crying until her parents agreed not to move
exercise: Ruby's parents got back together after a year apart, and she watches every argument for signs. What is true? | Trusting that things will stay good takes time | She is right to expect another split any day | Parents who split up once will always split up again | She should stop her parents from ever arguing
test: Maisie's mate, from Auckland: "Mum and Dad were rowing again last arvo. Bet it's cos I left me jandals out." ("Arvo" is afternoon; "jandals" are flip-flops.) What's the truth? | Grown-ups argue about grown-up things, not flip-flops | She's right; untidy kids are the main cause of rows | She should tidy everything away so that they never argue again | She should go in and stop the row herself
test: Josh's mate says, "two houses, two toothbrushes, that's well weird, it'll never feel normal." What usually happens? | Over months, a new kind of normal can feel okay | It never feels okay, not even after years | It goes back to one house again within a few weeks | He'll have to choose one house soon
test: A cartoon shows a kid making both divorced parents happy by secretly telling each one "I like you best". What's wrong with it? | Telling each parent different things makes it worse | Nothing; that's the best way to keep everyone happy | It's only wrong because the kid might get caught | Kids should always choose the richer parent
test: Leo thinks, "If I tell, Dad will get in trouble, so I'll keep it secret to protect him." What's the truth? | Telling a trusted adult can get the family help, and it's not his job to hide it | Keeping secrets about scary things at home is a kind way to protect parents | If he stays quiet it will stop on its own soon | Only grown-ups are allowed to tell anyone about home
test: Holly's nan, from the Midlands: "Don't be nesh about askin' for a pencil, duck." ("Nesh" means timid; "duck" means dear.) Why did Holly stop asking for things? | She wrongly blamed herself for the family's money worries | She had decided she didn't like pencils any more | Her mum had banned her from asking for anything at all, ever | She had saved up enough money to buy her own
test: What would happen if Max kept trying to cure his mum's depression on his own? | He'd wear himself out, because a child can't cure an illness | His jokes would make the illness go away completely | His mum would be better in a day or two | Nothing; depression doesn't need any help
test: Sophie shouts, "You're not my real dad!" What does the lesson say about this? | It's often said in anger; stepparents take time | It means she will never get on with him | It's a fair rule that only real dads can make rules | Stepdads should leave if they hear it once
test: Kian's mate says, "brothers always scrap, it's just rough-housing." ("Rough-housing" means play-fighting.) Is that right for Kian? | No; if it hurts and he's scared, it's not play | Yes; brothers can hit as hard as they like | Yes, as long as the bruises are hidden | No, but only parents can stop it, so he must wait
test: Mia says her dad lost his job because he was "rubbish at it". Which is true? | It usually means there was no work for the job | It always means the person was very bad at work | It means her dad chose to stop working | It means the family did something wrong
test: Ruby's cousin says, "they're back together, so you can be totally happy now, no worries." Why might Ruby feel happy and scared at once? | Trust takes time after a big change; mixed feelings are normal | She secretly wants them to split up again so she can have two homes | Being happy and scared at the very same time is impossible for anyone | She's scared only because her cousin said so, not because of anything real
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the cause of disputes between parents? | Such disputes concern adult matters and are not caused by the child | Such disputes are caused principally by the conduct of the child | Such disputes may be terminated by exemplary conduct on the part of the child | Such disputes arise solely where children are untidy at mealtimes
exam: It is given that a child is invited by a parent to elect with which parent he shall reside. Determine the response consistent with the established facts. | The determination is a matter for adults, and the child may decline to choose | The child is obliged to select the parent of greater means | The child should assure each parent privately of his preference | The child should withhold all affection until the matter is decided
exam: Identify the appropriate conduct of a child who experiences fear at home owing to a parent's excessive consumption of alcohol. | Disclosure to a trusted adult such as a teacher or a children's helpline | Concealment of the matter in order to protect the parent | Removal of the alcohol from the household without disclosure | Endurance of the situation pending its spontaneous resolution
exam: Ascertain which statement is consistent with the facts concerning depression in a parent. | It is an illness the child did not cause and cannot cure | It is a condition caused by insufficient humour on the part of the child | It is a deliberate choice which the parent may reverse at will | It is a matter which must be concealed from all other relatives
